WEBVTT

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.660
Hello everybody.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.900
Here is Tatiana Bazzichelli,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.980
the director of the Disruption Network Club,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.540
and I'm very happy to welcome all of you in Berlin

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.180
and also welcome the people that are following us online.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.060
This event is very important for us

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.100
because it's our 25th conference

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.940
and we are also launching our book,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.460
Whistleblowing for Change.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.060
The idea of these events was to collect

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.700
contributes from 30 speakers of the past

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.180
of the Disruption Network Club

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.900
and try to reflect on the impact of whistleblowing

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.340
on culture, politics, and society.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.540
And also thanks to the people that wrote in the book,

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.820
we got to know new people that also decided

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.700
to write for us on the book

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.980
and the panel that I'm going to introduce now

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.700
is actually a combination of some of our past speakers

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.700
and also new, very important people

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.180
that we got to know thanks to our network of trust.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.660
And so first I wanted to thank the audience here in Berlin

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.060
and also the audience online

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.980
and the reminder that we have a chat.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.540
So if they want to ask questions after

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.380
and during our introduction

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.300
and during the panel that they are welcome to do

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:51.300
and after the conversation we will then get your questions

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.780
so we can also bring them directly to the speakers.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.860
Just to give a bit of a context

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.100
of what we are doing here,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.780
we just watched the German premiere

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.500
of the film United States Versus Reality Winner.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.020
It's a film that was directed by Sonja Kennebeck

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.980
that we also have on stage with us

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:19.060
and it's a film about the NSA contractor Reality Winner

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.660
who disclosed a document

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:24.780
about Russian election interference to the media.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.580
Winner was arrested on June 3rd, 2017

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.580
and really this week she started a new phase at home

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.060
and I have to say that we know

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.020
that she is also not completely free

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.740
because there are a lot of restrictions on her

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.020
and we are going to learn also more

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.220
about the situation during this panel.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.060
But first I really wanted to thank Reality Winner

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.660
and Winner Davies for being with us this night

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.540
and I have to say it's really, really an honor for us

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.540
and I'm also so touched to see them at the distance

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.820
of course would have been wonderful to have them with us

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:46.640
but it's so great.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.160
And I have to say Reality and Billy

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.800
you really deserve this big applause

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.400
because here in Berlin everybody was really waiting

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.600
for these moments for many years actually

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.320
and I don't know, I'm so touched also personally

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.920
that I'm getting a bit emotional

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.080
because it's also really a big moment

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.360
for the Disruption Network Club

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.800
and so we just watched the film about your story

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.240
and I'm sure that many people have questions.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.480
Also I also want to really deeply thanks Billy

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.080
for the relationship we had in the past months

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.600
to write the book Contribution

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.840
that she also has on the book.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.720
So I really want to tell everybody read this

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.000
because it's a wonderful story

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.520
from the perspective of a mother

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.840
that also is fighting for justice

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.280
and the right of her daughter.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.200
So thank you Billy and also thank you Reality.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.560
I'm so happy to have you with us

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.040
and also want to welcome our speakers

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.880
and our moderator.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.000
First I start with John Kiriakou

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.920
that then will be introduced probably by Anna

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.600
but I want to say that John Kiriakou

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.360
was already with us in 2017

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.560
and in that specific event

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.680
we were also celebrating the release of Chelsea Manning.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.640
So actually today we were discussing

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.120
now you are back with us

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.520
and we are celebrating also the release of Reality Winner.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.360
So I mean these are very important moments

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.840
and it's really wonderful to have you back with us

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.080
and I already introduced before Sonia

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.520
but Anna will do it again

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.680
and I want to introduce Anna Myers

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.650
that is going to moderate this panel.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.570
So Anna Myers is the Executive Director

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:51.090
of the Whistleblowing International Network.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.650
She has worked in the field of whistleblowing for 20 years

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:58.050
advising individual whistleblowers, employers

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.810
of all sides and sector

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:03.050
and national international policy makers.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.050
Anna is originally from Canada

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:09.010
and was Deputy Director of Public Concern Network

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.290
for nine years now is called Protect

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.850
and has worked at Group of States Against Corruption

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.610
and at the Government Accountability Project

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.330
in Washington DC.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:23.290
So now I leave to you Anna, the conversation

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.890
and also then let's remind to call in

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:28.850
also the audience online.

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:29.770
Thank you.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.210
Thank you so much Tassiana.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:35.130
Well, it's such an honor to be here with all of you

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.930
and I think the one joy, well a private joy of mine

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.730
of being moderator is I get to ask questions first

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:45.690
and I'm so delighted to welcome Billy

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.930
and Reality to this event.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:51.250
We're sitting in a really lovely room

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:52.850
in the middle of Berlin

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:55.170
and it's in a gorgeous building

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.010
and we've got these kind of high ceilings

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:58.130
so there's a kind of echo here.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.330
So the applause for you was quite astounding

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:04.690
and was amplified very much though.

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:07.490
I wanted to let the audience know

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.450
and those who are just joining us

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:14.450
that Reality has finished her prison term this week

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:17.650
but she's still under some orders

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:19.050
and so there will be some limits

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.570
to what the questions are that people can ask her

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.610
or that she's allowed to talk about.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.250
Both Billy and Reality will let us know

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.290
when we've crossed those lines

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:28.930
but she basically cannot talk at all

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.010
about the classified information

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.810
and really about the case

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.610
but she can talk about her interests

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:37.810
and she'll let us know what she feels like speaking

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:39.650
if she would like to speak to us

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.810
and I will introduce briefly our panelists

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.450
because they can introduce themselves

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:45.970
so much better than I can.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:48.930
We have Sonia, who's Kennebec

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.850
who's the documentary director of this film.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:55.970
She has a long history of working

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.530
in the doing documentaries

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.970
and working in investigative journalism

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.250
and it really struck me

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.090
as I was watching the documentary

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.730
and a question I would have is

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:08.010
is it really a longer piece of investigative journalism

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.970
or is it an entire different beast really?

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:12.730
Because certainly what we all felt

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.530
as we watched this documentary today

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:16.370
or at least I can speak for myself

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:18.730
is the layers that you were able to weave in

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:20.570
of the personal stories,

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:22.810
the context in which this case

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:26.890
happened and also all the issues

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:28.970
that were raised around justice

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:31.890
and whistleblowers and how it works in practice

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.970
so we really had a full picture

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:37.210
and it raises I'm sure everybody has a lot of questions

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:40.770
and Sonia has done this work for a long time

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.530
and has really started to focus on whistleblowers

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:46.450
I think in the last three documentary films

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.610
that she's done in particular

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:51.370
and I'd really like to understand a little bit more

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:53.610
about why you started to become interested in this

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:54.970
and why you chose reality

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:57.290
after focusing on the drone program

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.690
and the whistleblowers there with National Bird

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.930
and with the hacking situation in the family

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:05.490
with public enemy or enemy of the state

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:07.090
so there's a lot to ask you there

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:10.770
John it's great to meet you again

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:14.010
and John you heard him in the film

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:17.290
very articulate about both the issues

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.090
that are raised by reality's case

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.370
but obviously has lived through the experience himself

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:24.650
as a former CIA employee

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.610
he's an illustrious career as well

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.850
and what really comes out from both his story

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.730
and reality story is you were hard workers

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.210
you did your jobs, you served your countries

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:37.650
and you were very dedicated to what you did

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.530
and then there was a point at which you felt

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:40.490
you needed to speak up

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:43.330
and there's a lot that happened after that

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:48.010
Billy you are both a mother and now an activist

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:51.570
an advocate you really were thrown into this

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.810
by being in a family of people

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:55.250
that were clearly dedicated

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:58.970
and I'd really like to ask both Billy and reality

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.770
if they'd like to start by perhaps making a statement

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:05.290
about your thoughts and feelings now

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:08.450
and what you've been dealing with recently

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:09.290
to the audience

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:10.930
and we'll let everyone have an opportunity

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.530
to make that statement to the audience

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:15.690
and then I'll ask a few questions

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:17.090
and then we'll open it up

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:18.930
so which one of you would like to start?

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:20.410
Billy would you like to start

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.410
or would reality like to say something?

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.250
Sure I'll go ahead and start

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:30.490
I want to say how grateful I am

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:34.330
that you all included us in this event

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:37.730
it was such an honor to be asked to contribute to the book

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:40.330
I've never been asked to contribute to a book

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:42.730
I've never been published

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.890
and so that was a challenge

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:47.970
and it was such an honor for me

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.450
and then this event here

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:51.050
this whistleblowing event

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.850
I was also very happy to see

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:57.130
that you guys were able to incorporate the documentary

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:01.850
United States versus reality winner into your program

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:04.330
and I'm happy that the audience there

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.290
and the people who are online

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:09.330
were able to actually see the documentary

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.450
to see what's happened in reality's case

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:16.930
to see the persecution of her by our government

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:18.730
what happened to her

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.970
just because she did the right thing

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:25.530
she released information to the American people

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.530
that she felt that we needed at a time

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.850
when we desperately needed it

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:33.570
and you know the reaction

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:36.170
of the United States government against her

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:39.570
is a story that really needs to be out there

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.250
needs to be told

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:42.690
I know as you said

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:46.410
I was not an activist before this

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:49.170
I still don't consider myself an activist

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.730
I still consider myself a mother

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:53.890
I consider myself a mother

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:56.770
who will continue to fight

00:12:56.770 --> 00:13:01.850
until I see that justice is done for my daughter

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:06.090
we continue to fight and ask for either clemency

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.450
for her sentence to end

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:10.210
or for a full pardon

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:13.610
so that she can be completely and totally free

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:14.810
at some point

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.210
to tell her side of this story

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.370
when I started this work

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:21.890
as far as being her voice

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:24.330
that's really what it was meant to be

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:26.610
because our government locked her up

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.770
and they silenced reality

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:31.370
and somebody needed to be her voice

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:34.810
somebody needed to tell her side and her story

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:38.650
and that's you know what I set out to do

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:41.050
I had so much help along the way

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.970
Sonia was a huge help in doing the documentary

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.890
there's also other people that helped me

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.850
and supported me along the way

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.610
and also supported reality through this

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:55.770
and I'm so very grateful for every single person

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:57.330
who has helped us

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:59.490
and you know gave me the strength

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.930
so that I didn't stop

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.170
the standing ovation that we received

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.290
when this started just kind of blew me away

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.690
because that was something I really wasn't expecting

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.370
I really am grateful to everyone there for that

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.050
Well, much, much deserved

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:22.410
and we're delighted you could be here

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:27.700
Reality, is there anything you'd like to say at this point?

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:28.980
I'm sure there will be answers

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:31.660
about my actual status later on

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.820
going forward in the restrictions

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:35.980
but for right now I just wanna say thank you

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:40.900
it's a surprise if I even find people in my own country

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:42.780
that care about my story

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.980
let alone all you find people in Berlin

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:48.140
so feeling the love from across the Atlantic

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:49.580
is really, really nice

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.020
it's really appreciated

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.020
and hi, Sonia

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.780
So for those of you who may have just joined us online

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.460
we've just watched this incredible documentary

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:08.180
about a young woman who was arrested

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:12.620
and then charged under the Espionage Act in the US

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:16.060
she was charged with releasing to the media

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:19.660
a single document that actually showed

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:23.740
and was about Russian interference in the 2016 elections

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.540
this was at a time as all of us will recall

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:29.460
doesn't seem very long ago at all

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:34.340
where we were from afar watching the elections in the US

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.940
and Donald Trump was elected

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:38.900
and one of the things that really comes out

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:42.300
in the documentary is the context

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:46.540
in which the prosecution was taking place

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.260
and the fact that

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:52.100
and there's lots of issues around how she was treated

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.420
by those who were arresting her and her rights

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:57.460
and then of course the courtroom setting

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.140
and the Espionage Act is something

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:02.420
I hope John will give us a little run through on

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:05.100
but it's a very draconian law

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:08.220
and it is not fit for what it's being used for

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.740
and it gives people no chance for a defense

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.380
and it's one of the ones that is really

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:17.940
where the whistle-blowing issue is starkly in play

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.700
because you're talking about classification,

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:23.660
national security, the state

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:25.140
and you're talking about the public's right

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:26.460
to know in the public interest

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:30.140
and we have this discussion that

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:32.860
or actually I would say a kind of argument

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:36.980
that often ensues where people are called traitors or heroes

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.660
and most of us need to think

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:40.860
in a much more nuanced way about this

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.380
and the question that I tend to ask myself

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:44.980
is did I want to know

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:46.500
and if it's a yes I did

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:48.460
well then why didn't other people tell us

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:49.780
and if we need whistle-blowers

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:51.820
why do we need them it's a big question

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:53.060
and we should be celebrating them

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:55.420
if we can't get the information any other way.

00:16:55.420 --> 00:17:00.060
Sonja, you've been going down this road now for a while

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.380
and I know that you worked in Berlin for many years

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:05.860
and your roots are international

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:08.460
but German and now you're in New York as well.

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:10.860
It was a question I had.

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:12.780
You know, is there a big difference

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:14.780
in making a documentary

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:16.260
than doing an investigative reporter?

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.020
Is it longer investigations

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:20.100
and would you like to explain how you got

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:22.380
what made you choose this story

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:28.310
and how you got here?

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:32.430
So, you know, because we as a team

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.790
we've been focusing on whistle-blower stories

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:38.990
it's really wonderful to have the German Premier

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:41.430
at the whistle-blowering for a change conference.

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:46.510
With an audience that understands

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:47.790
what whistle-blowering means

00:17:47.790 --> 00:17:50.470
and people, many of you I know

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:54.110
who are advocating for a wider public

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:56.190
to understand what whistle-blowering means

00:17:56.190 --> 00:18:00.630
which is exposing ways fraud, abuse,

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.910
government misconduct in the public interest

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.630
and I think more people have to know that

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.870
and I think everyone here

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:11.990
has been doing incredible educational work.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:15.630
It is very emotional for me to see this film

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.270
with an audience but also, you know,

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:22.790
in the film we are documenting the four years

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:26.950
that reality was spending imprisoned

00:18:26.950 --> 00:18:31.110
and to see reality next to Billy

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:33.670
free right now in the house

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:35.990
or almost, you know, hopefully,

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:37.750
hopefully someday really free

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:42.030
but closer to freedom is really, really wonderful

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.430
and I can actually see it in the eyes

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:50.030
of my team members who are here right now,

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:52.710
Torsten Lapp, the director of photography

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:55.230
and Maxine Gurdike, the editor

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:59.310
that, you know, they are really moved as well

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:04.230
and yeah, I actually want to start by thanking reality.

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:07.190
Reality, thank you for your courage

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:09.870
but also your trust in us

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:13.110
when you were in a really vulnerable position

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.550
and probably, you know, everyone would have understood

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:19.430
if you didn't have any trust in journalists

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:21.110
or filmmakers at that point

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:25.670
and you gave us access and trust

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:27.870
to document and tell your story

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:31.750
together with Billy Winner-Davis and Gary Davis

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:35.230
and your sister, Brittany Winner-Davis

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:39.070
and yeah, we are very, very thankful for that

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:43.510
and I really think that people take away from this film

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:47.230
not to be more scared

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:52.070
but to be inspired by the courage of reality

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.190
and her family as well

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:57.150
and really, you know, push back

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:58.990
against government suppression

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:01.870
so thank you very much, reality and Billy

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:06.030
and yeah, and I also, because, you know,

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.390
filmmaking is teamwork

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:13.870
and especially when you do independent documentaries

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:16.270
it's incredibly hard.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.710
It's really, you were just asking the question

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:20.790
about, you know, the difference between

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:23.710
an investigative report and a documentary

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:25.750
and the difference is really

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:29.310
that an investigative documentary

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:31.990
has all these layers, you know,

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:34.430
you have to do the fundraising

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:38.870
to, yeah, to, you know, cover the travel expenses

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:42.870
and really be able to document a development

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:45.430
like this over years

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:49.750
and we felt so, you know,

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:52.030
we felt it was incredibly important

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:53.590
after I first, you know,

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:55.910
went to one of the first pre-trial hearings

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:57.590
to start filming and documenting

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:00.270
because there was so little media coverage.

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.550
I expected there would be, you know,

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:06.910
just camera crews outside and national reporters

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.670
and that, you know, everyone would cover this case

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:12.590
because not only was it, you know,

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:15.590
a national security whistleblower

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:19.430
and NSA whistleblower after Edward Snowden,

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.910
you know, disclose this information

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:25.990
about, you know, mass surveillance and data collection

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:29.310
but it was about election interference

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.550
and there was so much, you know,

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:34.070
it was such a contentious issue at that time

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:37.670
so I just expected that there would be such broad coverage

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:39.750
and when that didn't happen,

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:44.510
I, you know, I spoke to my producing partner,

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:47.550
you know, the day after I was at the pre-trial hearing

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.190
Ines Hofmann-Kena and I said to her, you know,

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:52.510
I thought there were many more journalists here

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:53.510
and it's not the case

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:55.830
and we just decided on the spot

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:57.270
that we have to witness it.

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:59.350
We have to pay witness, we have to document

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:03.070
because this was a historic case.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:07.470
So Ines Hofmann-Kena, my producing partner,

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:11.910
she has, you know, produced all of my independent films

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.510
then the shoots that we did, the first one

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.550
and all the subsequent shoots were

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:19.750
with Torsten Lapp, who's over there,

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:22.470
can, you know, could you stand up for me

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:25.190
for a moment, director of photography,

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:26.270
person Lapp?

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:31.410
Torsten Lapp?

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:34.130
Torsten is, you know, we've worked together

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:36.490
for a very long time, over 15 years.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:39.170
We started ARD's Panorama

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:43.930
and we filmed all of the independent documentaries

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:45.530
long and short together

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:49.970
and Torsten is someone who's never intimidated.

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:52.050
He is never deterred.

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:56.370
He, when you see these few rare images of reality,

00:22:57.330 --> 00:22:59.730
you know, where she was, where they were just,

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:03.850
you know, taking her into the van and the car,

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:08.050
he was the one running behind the car and the van,

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:10.970
you know, just trying to capture an image of her

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:14.410
because they just, you know, they locked her away

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:19.410
and it was important for us to show who she is

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:22.170
and then we were sitting for two days

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.570
in a 100 degree seat in the car

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:29.610
trying to capture these rare images of reality in the jail

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:34.610
and yeah, Torsten is really someone who has a lot of courage

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:37.210
but also respect and compassion

00:23:37.210 --> 00:23:41.210
and then Maxine Goetheke, the editor of this film

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:44.130
and all of my other independent films.

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:51.570
Maxine, could you please stand up as well?

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:54.290
So you might have seen this film

00:23:54.290 --> 00:23:57.610
is a film that really shows the art

00:23:57.650 --> 00:23:59.570
and the craft of an editor

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:03.410
and Maxine is an amazing storyteller.

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:07.530
She is a fantastic researcher who went through all

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:12.530
of the social media and, you know, really found

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:18.570
what I hope captures sort of the essence

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:23.570
of reality's humor and, you know, courage

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:28.730
and outspokenness and yeah, and also, you know,

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:34.650
throughout this journey really has so much compassion

00:24:36.410 --> 00:24:39.130
and respect for the lead characters,

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:42.490
the protagonist whom she often meets, you know,

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:47.010
after the film is edited and laughs in Christ with them

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:50.930
and yeah, and really is so, you know,

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:55.930
has this, I don't know, the humanity to bring across

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:01.130
such a beautiful story that really goes through the heart,

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:03.810
I think, hopefully for all of you

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:07.170
and then in Zarudo of the composer

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:10.010
who also wrote the credit song

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:13.810
which is inspired by a reality winners poem

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:18.010
and then, you know, I also want to mention Marin Poitras,

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:20.330
not related to Laura Poitras

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.090
who was an associate producer

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:26.610
and who was also the actress in our recreations

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:30.570
so this was our very small independent team

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:31.970
who put together this film

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:36.970
and I couldn't do my work without them, so thank you.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:40.410
Well, well done, you, all of you, thank you

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:42.330
and it does strike me that there's a number

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.530
of women involved in these projects

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:46.570
and there's, you know, looking at reality

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:50.090
and her mom seeing that as a dream team as well.

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:53.130
There's a lot of women talking about these issues

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:54.730
and working in the field of whistleblowing

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:59.090
and it strikes me that there is a real moment

00:25:59.090 --> 00:26:01.290
of needing to consider how power is operating

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:02.130
and how it's working

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:05.370
and women do sometimes take the time to do that

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:08.850
and that may be part of what's going on, John.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:11.890
I can just let you speak

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:15.050
but I do want you to,

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:16.970
well, I'm really interested in,

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:18.890
you said, well, we were listening to this,

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:21.530
just how many layers there are

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:24.810
and this is some of the nuancing

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:27.170
that just gets lost in whistleblower stories,

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:29.450
especially with this narrative of hero or traitor

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.570
and you just go down that road.

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:34.850
These are real people and they have real lives

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:38.370
and they are silly and funny and get on with things

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:41.490
and make mistakes just the same as the rest of us

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:43.090
and blowing the whistle

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:46.050
where you do get into hot water

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:48.610
with whether it's your employer directly

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:52.290
or the courts or the media affects the whole family.

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:55.330
So one of the things that comes through in this film

00:26:55.330 --> 00:26:58.410
is it's a family project

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.530
but it also can have, it just does

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:03.970
and I know that that's a burden for many of the people

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:07.570
who have done these actions and they feel it

00:27:07.570 --> 00:27:10.690
so I would love you to also mention a little bit about that.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:11.530
Thanks for that.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:13.930
There's so much to say.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:16.010
I almost don't know where to begin.

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:21.010
I think I will begin by saying that the relationship

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:25.850
between Billy and reality is such a beautiful

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:30.250
and powerful thing and it's also very unusual.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:34.490
You'd be surprised how often the government

00:27:34.490 --> 00:27:38.050
will approach the spouse of a whistleblower

00:27:38.050 --> 00:27:41.930
and say you're either with us or you're with them

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:45.130
because oftentimes we work in the same agencies, right?

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.130
Tonight is about reality

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:50.490
but I wanna tell one very short story.

00:27:51.770 --> 00:27:54.650
My marriage didn't survive my whistleblowing

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:58.770
and I found myself involved in a custody dispute

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:03.490
which is not unusual and so I was in court recently

00:28:03.490 --> 00:28:06.410
and my attorney asked me on the stand

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:07.890
was she a good wife?

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:10.010
And I said she was an amazing wife.

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:12.850
When I blew the whistle on the CIA's torture program

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:16.610
she went with me to the interview and sat just off camera

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:18.810
and when I finished I said how did I do?

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:20.810
She said you did great.

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:24.170
I said I didn't say anything classified, right?

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:26.970
And she said no, you did great.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.570
And then her attorney called her up to the stand

00:28:29.570 --> 00:28:31.970
and said is it true that you went with him

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:35.050
when he blew the whistle on the CIA's torture program?

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:36.890
And she said yes.

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:39.810
And her attorney said why did you do that?

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.570
And she said because the CIA's Office of Security

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:44.090
instructed me to go with him

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:46.770
and then to report back to them on his activities.

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:50.090
So to see these two together

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:54.890
and to see the love and the connection and the support

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:58.170
that's exactly the way that it's supposed to be.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:01.690
That wasn't my experience.

00:29:01.690 --> 00:29:07.660
Yeah, it was theirs.

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:09.900
I'm sorry to say that Tom Drake

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:12.580
and we're all such great friends

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:14.780
and in this very small whistleblower community

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:16.340
it's like we have a club.

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:19.420
We have dinner together

00:29:19.420 --> 00:29:23.820
and one time I dragged FBI surveillance

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:26.100
to a dinner with Tom Drake

00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:29.780
and he dragged FBI surveillance to the dinner with me.

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:32.940
And so we went out to go to our cars together

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:34.980
and he said which one is with you?

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:36.660
I said I think this one's with me.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:38.900
I don't know this one, he must be with you.

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:40.580
And then they followed us home.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.380
But anyway, that's the way the relationship

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:45.260
is supposed to be.

00:29:45.260 --> 00:29:47.940
You know, we're all there for one another.

00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:49.740
We're all there to support one another.

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:53.380
We're there to learn from previous whistleblowers

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.380
and the experiences that they had.

00:29:58.060 --> 00:30:03.060
One of the things that I'm proudest of in my life

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:09.340
is that Ed Snowden told the New York Times

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:11.660
that Tom Drake and I inspired him

00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:14.620
to go public with his revelations.

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:17.220
The same thing is gonna be said of reality winner.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:20.100
One of the things that Ed said in the film

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:24.780
is so true and so powerful, it bears repeating.

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:29.820
Reality's sentence isn't gonna deter anybody.

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.220
No sentence is gonna deter anybody.

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:33.620
There was a wonderful study done

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:37.620
by an Israeli journalist named Al Press

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:43.140
about four whistleblowers in modern world history,

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:44.780
20th century whistleblowers.

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:46.260
And one of the things that he found,

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:48.100
one of the threads that he found

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:50.460
that ran through all of these cases

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:53.500
was that whistleblowers have an unusually

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:56.820
highly defined sense of right and wrong.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:31:00.660
Much more highly defined than the general public.

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:04.740
And he also found that there is no such thing

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:06.500
as a deterrent.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:09.460
You get a year, two years, five, 10 years in prison.

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:13.020
It doesn't matter because a whistleblower

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:16.100
can't sleep at night recognizing

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:18.500
that there's an injustice out there

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:21.780
about which he or she could do something.

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:24.500
I think reality would agree with me

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:27.980
when I say that on my very first day at the CIA,

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:29.860
I put my right hand in the air

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:32.420
and I promised to protect the Constitution

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:35.420
of the United States against all enemies,

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:37.660
foreign and domestic.

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:42.660
I hate to think that of the 300 people in the room that day,

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:45.140
I was the only one who actually meant it.

00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:48.660
I know that reality meant it,

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:51.180
Tom Drake meant it, Ed Snowden meant it.

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.860
So whistleblowers are out there

00:31:54.860 --> 00:31:58.380
and it's up to us to encourage them,

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:00.660
to help to protect them,

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:04.460
and God forbid that they're arrested to support them.

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:16.060
This film, frankly, was a giant leap in that direction.

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:18.620
Thank you, John, and we'll come back to you.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:21.700
Billy, one of the things that I did want to ask you

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:25.860
and that sort of comes up a few times in your chapter

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:28.620
as well, which I did get a chance to read

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:32.740
and as you were talking in the documentary film,

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:36.520
there wasn't a lot of public support.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.160
There wasn't a lot of media attention

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:40.800
to get that public support,

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:44.440
but we also heard elected officials and people in Congress

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:46.600
saying they wouldn't have known it

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:49.080
if they hadn't had the information

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:53.080
and that the substance of it was clearly important.

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.240
And at the same time, at the time

00:32:55.240 --> 00:32:57.840
that they were relying on this information,

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:00.000
they were making it a point to say,

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.720
but we condemn whoever leaked it

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:03.880
and we don't agree with leaks.

00:33:03.880 --> 00:33:06.160
And I'll come to John in a minute again,

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:08.360
but I just wanted to ask you a little bit more

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:10.120
about what was going on

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:14.520
and what are your theory's opinions about that,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:17.680
about the lack of interest publicly

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.880
and then this need to say out loud

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:22.480
at the same time, thank God we know this.

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:24.000
Well, we shouldn't have known it this way,

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:28.280
but condemning the leaker.

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:32.160
Yeah, so I mean, that was one of the hardest things

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:35.080
throughout the last four years or so

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:38.080
was just trying to get support

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:43.800
and at least get support from any of our congressional leaders.

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:45.940
And we still to this day,

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:49.440
do not have the support of not one congressional leader.

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:52.400
And Sonya I know included in the film,

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:56.360
the piece where I think it was Claire McCaskill said

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:58.080
that she condemns the whistleblower,

00:33:58.080 --> 00:33:59.440
but yet at the same time,

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:02.600
they were using the very information

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:06.240
that my daughter released to do their jobs

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:08.800
and they wouldn't have had access to it

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:10.440
had it not been released.

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:12.960
We had other reports where the states

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:16.000
that had been jeopardized did not even know

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:17.400
because nobody told them

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:20.360
until they found out about it through this leak.

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:24.720
And to me, I mean, that is just very telling

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:26.880
about the problems that we have

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:29.360
with our government in the United States.

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:33.160
When the government has gotten too big and too powerful

00:34:33.160 --> 00:34:37.600
and they are keeping information from the American people

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:40.480
and you see it through reality's case

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:43.360
where the information when it gets out there

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:46.720
is so vital and so important and so useful

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:49.800
to all of these groups who are investigating this,

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:52.440
yet nobody will come to the defense

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:55.760
or support the person who actually gave them

00:34:55.760 --> 00:35:00.440
the information because nobody wants to support.

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:04.160
I think that probably the charge of the Espionage Act

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:07.960
is something that played into that

00:35:07.960 --> 00:35:11.080
because nobody wants to come to the defense

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:15.160
of somebody who's being accused of espionage.

00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:17.880
And I think also in our country

00:35:17.880 --> 00:35:19.680
that's something that needs to be worked on

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:23.800
is reform to get away from allowing our country

00:35:23.800 --> 00:35:28.800
to use the Espionage Act against the people who release

00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:32.920
the truth when we need it and people who blow the whistle

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:35.320
because that is something that I saw

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:38.960
that kept a lot of the support away from reality

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:41.740
because of that severe charge against her.

00:35:42.920 --> 00:35:45.200
Do you think also the timing

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:49.680
because it was at the time that Trump had just been elected

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:53.200
do you think that there was some awareness

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:55.760
in terms of, I don't know, in the air

00:35:55.760 --> 00:35:58.320
that if you were gonna say something and be critical

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:01.360
and agree that this information should have been out there

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:04.200
you wanted to sort of say out loud, just in case,

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:06.360
a little bit like I do believe in God

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:11.520
that I do condemn leakers

00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:15.200
just so that that wasn't gonna be brought up against them.

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:17.880
Do you think there was something about the context

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:20.740
at the time that would be different now or not?

00:36:21.740 --> 00:36:24.220
I don't think it's any different right now

00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:27.980
because we're still not seeing any kind of support

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:31.600
for reality or other whistleblowers.

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:35.940
I was hopeful through Trump's administration

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:39.660
when leakers were just like a daily thing

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:42.940
and people were actually celebrating whistleblowers

00:36:42.940 --> 00:36:45.220
and celebrating leakers

00:36:45.220 --> 00:36:48.180
but yet that didn't stop our government

00:36:48.180 --> 00:36:50.460
from persecuting them.

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:53.180
We saw other people who were charged

00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:56.180
and who went to prison during this time

00:36:56.180 --> 00:37:01.180
and again, no one in our Congress

00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:06.300
will come to the aid or the support of a whistleblower

00:37:06.340 --> 00:37:10.100
and actually support them.

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:15.220
John, you had Senator John McCain stand up on the floor

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:18.580
and say that the public owed you a debt of gratitude.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:19.740
I was very fortunate.

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:22.300
Yeah, what's, where's the difference

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:25.340
or was it, yeah, if you wouldn't mind commenting.

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:31.140
I was, I don't wanna say I was fortunate

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:35.660
because that's really what it came down to.

00:37:38.140 --> 00:37:40.540
After I blew the whistle and before I was arrested

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:41.980
I went to work for John Kerry

00:37:41.980 --> 00:37:44.700
on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee staff

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:48.140
and Kerry's office in the Russell Senate Office building

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:51.060
was next door to McCain's office.

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:54.500
And so every time we would run into McCain in the hall

00:37:54.500 --> 00:37:58.140
he would make a point, he would go out of his way

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.500
to come and shake my hand and say good morning,

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:01.340
good afternoon.

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:03.700
And I'd say good morning, Senator, nice to see you.

00:38:03.700 --> 00:38:05.180
Finally, Kerry said to me,

00:38:05.180 --> 00:38:08.140
why don't you two get a room or something?

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:10.700
And I said, no, it's the torture thing.

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:12.740
He's genuine.

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:16.060
He's really, he had reputation

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:17.980
for being the meanest man in Washington.

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:21.220
He was always nice to me, very nice.

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:24.780
And then when I got arrested, like Billy says,

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:27.320
official Washington goes completely silent.

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:31.140
Well, except for John McCain.

00:38:32.060 --> 00:38:35.340
And when the Senate Torture Report finally came out

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:37.860
six weeks before I was released from prison

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:41.700
and proved that everything I said had been true,

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:45.780
McCain went to the floor of the Senate and gave a speech

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:49.580
and said that the country owed me a debt of gratitude

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:52.380
because if I hadn't gone public,

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:54.860
they would never, the American people would never have known

00:38:54.860 --> 00:38:57.100
what their country was doing in their name.

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:00.660
And then he said privately

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:02.540
that he was gonna help me get my pension back.

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:06.500
Part of my sentence was that I lost my federal pension

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:10.420
and I had worked for 20 years in the federal government.

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:14.980
I had saved $770,000 in that pension.

00:39:14.980 --> 00:39:17.260
I'm gonna have to work till the day I die.

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:19.460
I can't afford to ever retire.

00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:24.260
And just weeks after he said,

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:26.780
I'm gonna help you get that pension back,

00:39:26.780 --> 00:39:29.340
he got brain cancer and then died.

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:31.740
And that was it.

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:33.220
Nobody else on Capitol Hill

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:35.620
has ever said so much as good morning to me.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:39.860
It's tough finding official support,

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:41.260
tough to impossible.

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:44.980
I wanted to come back to you, Sonia.

00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:46.780
Yeah, there's a lot to take in.

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:51.940
You really did put it so succinctly

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:53.260
at the beginning of your talk

00:39:53.260 --> 00:39:55.140
that you were in that courtroom

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:57.300
and there weren't other people there

00:39:57.300 --> 00:39:59.620
that needed to be there to start reporting on this.

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:02.100
And do you have some theories about that

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:05.220
having then been with reality

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:07.980
and Billy for a long time after that?

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:11.900
I've been thinking about this a lot.

00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:16.780
Why is there so little reporting on whistleblower stories?

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:19.580
I think in reality, winners case,

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:22.540
one of the reasons was also that it was not

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:24.780
in the Washington DC area

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:28.940
where you have a lot of national media

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:31.620
and especially national security reporters

00:40:31.620 --> 00:40:35.620
and it is a little bit easier to get to.

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:40.300
I think that most of publications

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:44.700
or networks in the US really look at their bottom line

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.580
and the travel expenses

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:51.460
and especially in a pre-trial hearing like this,

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:54.780
you can't take cameras into the courtroom.

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:59.780
You don't get access to reality herself

00:41:02.100 --> 00:41:04.820
and I think they might have thought

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:06.700
that they were not getting enough,

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:09.620
just standing outside the courtroom

00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:12.340
and not having access, of course,

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:15.260
limits what people can report about

00:41:15.260 --> 00:41:16.980
and I think they do that on purpose

00:41:16.980 --> 00:41:21.100
and I also think they really worked very, very hard

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:23.380
to keep reality behind bars.

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:25.340
I actually never expected

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:28.580
that I would have to do another film

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:31.740
with an absent character,

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:34.300
an absent lead character protagonist

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:36.700
because I just thought at the beginning,

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:39.420
oh, she's going to be let out on bail

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:40.940
and we can have a conversation

00:41:40.940 --> 00:41:43.380
and reality winner is going to tell her own story

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:45.420
and this just never happened

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:49.380
but I did not want to make this a reason

00:41:49.380 --> 00:41:51.020
to not make a film

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:53.620
because it was even more important,

00:41:53.620 --> 00:41:55.500
I failed to get her story out

00:41:55.500 --> 00:41:58.260
when she wasn't able to speak for herself

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:00.900
and yeah, I think the other reason is too

00:42:00.940 --> 00:42:03.740
that there is sort of this fear

00:42:03.740 --> 00:42:07.940
of some major media organizations to lose access

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:12.020
to official press conferences.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:14.660
I think there is pressure from the government

00:42:14.660 --> 00:42:18.500
to not report on these type of national security

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:20.140
whistleblower stories

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:22.820
because they don't want to,

00:42:22.820 --> 00:42:26.260
they might think and maybe you know more about it

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:29.140
but I could imagine that the government

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:30.700
is all about deterrence,

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:33.020
they don't want to incentivize anything

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:36.300
so they just don't want to have reporting about it

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:39.140
and yeah, so the corporate media,

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:43.020
afraid of losing access

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:45.700
and I think there's also an element of self-censorship

00:42:45.700 --> 00:42:47.860
which I find extremely dangerous

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:51.940
where the general public but also reporters

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:55.500
are getting increasingly more scared and worried

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:57.860
about touching these type of stories

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:00.940
because they think, oh, maybe I can become a target,

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:03.740
maybe I shouldn't be asking this type of question,

00:43:03.740 --> 00:43:04.940
I actually have heard,

00:43:04.940 --> 00:43:06.540
even when I have been giving interviews,

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:10.340
I've heard journalists say to me before,

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:12.220
oh, after they asked me a question,

00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:14.380
oh, maybe I shouldn't be asking this type of question,

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:16.980
I'm like, you're just asking a question.

00:43:16.980 --> 00:43:18.780
Don't start self-censoring

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:21.340
and I think that is probably the most dangerous.

00:43:22.340 --> 00:43:28.340
Yeah, it is affecting reporting around the world

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:31.340
and certainly some of what we were mentioning

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:33.020
just at the end of the documentary,

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:36.940
we're sitting beside each other about some of the fact

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:39.100
that the sentences are getting higher.

00:43:39.100 --> 00:43:41.860
Before I move, I do want to come back to the Espionage Act

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:44.140
but I just wanted to ask reality,

00:43:44.140 --> 00:43:46.340
if she might want to comment because I know,

00:43:46.340 --> 00:43:49.100
I understand that you may not have seen the documentary

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:53.020
which is fine, you've had a lot to deal with

00:43:53.020 --> 00:43:58.020
and just to return to be with your family.

00:43:58.180 --> 00:44:02.220
You were, I think, released to home confinement in June,

00:44:02.220 --> 00:44:03.060
is that right?

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.140
Have you been back with your mom since June

00:44:05.140 --> 00:44:07.340
or have you only been back with her

00:44:07.340 --> 00:44:10.620
and Gary since this week?

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:15.460
I've actually been on home confinement since June 9th

00:44:15.460 --> 00:44:19.300
with an ankle monitor and constant phone calls

00:44:19.300 --> 00:44:22.380
and threats of escape.

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:26.300
I ventured too far into my living room one day

00:44:26.300 --> 00:44:28.660
and they told me I was escape status.

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:33.660
Just absolutely nerve-wracking

00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:35.820
the way they could levy these threats

00:44:35.820 --> 00:44:37.340
but they never documented.

00:44:37.340 --> 00:44:42.180
So we had actually no accountability

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:45.180
or ways to address how we could prevent

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:47.980
the constant trauma from reoccurring

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.220
because then I would speak to the director

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:53.300
of the halfway house or my case manager

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:56.340
and they'd say, well, they never heard about it.

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:58.580
Meanwhile, I've had two panic attacks

00:44:58.580 --> 00:45:01.180
and I'm physically ill from the stress

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:04.980
because they told me I would be charged with escape

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:06.100
the night before.

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:10.380
So yes, I have been home

00:45:10.380 --> 00:45:11.940
but I haven't really been home.

00:45:11.980 --> 00:45:16.860
It's been a constant sort of pressure on my family.

00:45:16.860 --> 00:45:19.980
We've had back to back to back drug tests

00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:23.220
every other week for five months

00:45:23.220 --> 00:45:26.060
but I haven't had any approval to drive.

00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:29.780
So that means, you know, mom or dad has to take me

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:31.900
to go get my drug test

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:35.220
and it's 100 miles round trip.

00:45:35.220 --> 00:45:38.420
So it is a huge inconvenience

00:45:38.420 --> 00:45:40.420
and you would think, okay, well,

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:44.180
the feds wanna test me and they wanna do it effectively.

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:45.820
Come to my house.

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:48.500
Come get a urine sample at my house.

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:51.700
It should be on them, but instead it fell to me

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:55.500
and the ankle monitor buzzed constantly

00:45:55.500 --> 00:45:58.460
regardless of where I was, it never really worked.

00:46:00.580 --> 00:46:04.740
I went grocery shopping twice in five months

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:07.420
because every time I got approval

00:46:07.420 --> 00:46:09.660
they would suddenly pull approval

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:13.980
because for some reason if we were on home confinement

00:46:13.980 --> 00:46:16.260
and they felt like COVID was getting bad

00:46:16.260 --> 00:46:18.820
we were no longer allowed to go to restaurants

00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:22.500
or go grocery shopping.

00:46:22.500 --> 00:46:24.980
So I actually only went out to eat, I believe two

00:46:24.980 --> 00:46:27.020
or three times in five months.

00:46:28.820 --> 00:46:31.420
It just so happened COVID would tend to get bad

00:46:31.420 --> 00:46:34.300
whenever we had family down for the weekend

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:36.060
and so they would belatedly tell me

00:46:36.060 --> 00:46:39.760
that's why my social passes were disapproved the day of.

00:46:41.140 --> 00:46:43.960
It was just a lot of coincidences like that.

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:46.700
So on Tuesday, this past Tuesday

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:48.900
I had my ankle monitor cut off

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:51.820
and I was released to go meet with my probation officer

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:55.460
and he tells me for the next three years

00:46:55.460 --> 00:46:59.440
courtesy of my district in Georgia that sentenced me.

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:02.860
I have a 10 p.m. to 6 a.m. curfew,

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:06.640
365 days a year for the next three years.

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:09.980
That means no overnight trips no matter what.

00:47:09.980 --> 00:47:13.100
No late night movies, nothing.

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:18.180
It also means I have to have special permission

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:22.020
to leave work earlier than 6 a.m.

00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:26.900
I cannot leave the Southern District of Texas

00:47:26.900 --> 00:47:29.700
but even if I could I would have to keep it

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:33.660
within a five hour drive so I can make it back in time

00:47:33.660 --> 00:47:35.780
for that 10 p.m. curfew.

00:47:35.780 --> 00:47:39.540
So they have without saying so directly

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:42.340
have effectively disabled me

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:44.380
from any type of meaningful travel.

00:47:45.740 --> 00:47:47.500
That was a really crushing blow.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:51.100
You know, I have a godson in Wisconsin

00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:52.600
that I would like to go see.

00:47:54.140 --> 00:47:55.820
My sister lives in North Carolina

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:59.300
and I just thought that maybe for the first time

00:47:59.300 --> 00:48:01.100
in four and a half years I could bear

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:04.760
some of the burden for my family and go see them.

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:10.940
But now it's just being confined to this local area

00:48:10.940 --> 00:48:14.340
for the next three years makes it really hard to feel how

00:48:14.340 --> 00:48:15.900
how is this a step forward?

00:48:17.780 --> 00:48:20.020
We have been using these same two clauses

00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:23.780
from my plea agreement to sort of guide the way

00:48:23.780 --> 00:48:26.900
to how I can speak to the media

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:28.580
and speak out on my own behalf

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:33.180
and finally advocate for myself and start over.

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:38.020
And on Tuesday somehow this probation officer's

00:48:38.020 --> 00:48:41.100
interpretation even though the word interview

00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:46.100
is not stated not one time in my court restrictions

00:48:47.860 --> 00:48:50.540
for probation he just kept saying

00:48:50.540 --> 00:48:54.980
well as long as you don't do interviews it'll be fine.

00:48:54.980 --> 00:48:57.820
So I don't know what to do moving forward.

00:48:57.820 --> 00:49:01.580
I don't know what the ramifications

00:49:01.580 --> 00:49:04.180
of even a discussion like this will be.

00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:06.620
So I'm happy to be here, I'm happy to be heard

00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:12.380
but it just seemed like damn if I do, damn if I don't.

00:49:12.380 --> 00:49:15.740
If I stay silent the abuse will continue

00:49:15.740 --> 00:49:20.980
and if I speak out the abuse will continue.

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:23.780
Yeah, it's a really tough position to be in

00:49:23.780 --> 00:49:28.700
and maybe I could ask John a bit about this espionage act

00:49:28.740 --> 00:49:33.740
and how it operates but this seems a new twist.

00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:38.780
That's unusually harsh and I wonder if it's

00:49:39.620 --> 00:49:43.180
because it's Texas instead of one of the districts

00:49:43.180 --> 00:49:44.420
in the DC area.

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:47.580
I don't know when I first got out of prison reality

00:49:47.580 --> 00:49:52.580
I had a job offer at one of the oldest progressive

00:49:52.900 --> 00:49:54.220
think tanks in Washington,

00:49:54.220 --> 00:49:56.220
the Institute for Policy Studies.

00:49:57.180 --> 00:50:01.380
And they wanted me to think the big thoughts

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:04.500
and write papers and talk about prison reform

00:50:04.500 --> 00:50:06.700
and sentencing reform and things like that

00:50:06.700 --> 00:50:09.420
and I accepted the position.

00:50:09.420 --> 00:50:13.140
I sent it to my whatever they called themselves

00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:18.140
case officers, whatever and they rejected it.

00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:21.620
And I said, wait a minute, you told me

00:50:21.620 --> 00:50:25.140
that if I don't get a job I'm gonna go back to prison.

00:50:25.140 --> 00:50:29.220
So I got a job and you say I can't do it.

00:50:29.220 --> 00:50:31.060
Why can't I do it?

00:50:31.060 --> 00:50:34.980
And they said that it would be inappropriate

00:50:34.980 --> 00:50:39.980
for me to comment on sentencing reform.

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:43.660
And I said, that's a violation

00:50:43.660 --> 00:50:46.660
of my constitutional right to free speech.

00:50:46.660 --> 00:50:50.280
I'm gonna accept the job and if you wanna send me

00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:53.580
back to prison I'm sure CNN and others

00:50:53.580 --> 00:50:56.420
would love to ask you plenty of questions, why?

00:50:57.420 --> 00:51:00.750
And they backed off.

00:51:00.750 --> 00:51:03.990
My experience with reporting to the halfway house

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:05.590
is the same as yours.

00:51:05.590 --> 00:51:07.830
My halfway house was only seven miles away

00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:11.030
as the crow flies but I wasn't allowed to drive

00:51:11.030 --> 00:51:12.190
just like you.

00:51:12.190 --> 00:51:15.270
So I had to walk a half an hour to the subway,

00:51:15.270 --> 00:51:17.630
take the subway to Capitol Hill

00:51:17.630 --> 00:51:20.790
and then get a bus that took another hour

00:51:20.790 --> 00:51:23.390
to get to the halfway house which was in

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:28.150
the worst block in the worst neighborhood in Washington.

00:51:28.150 --> 00:51:30.750
When I got off the bus one time a guy directly

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:33.470
across the street shot and killed another guy

00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:36.510
and I jumped back onto the bus to protect myself,

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:38.470
the killer ran away and then I walked

00:51:38.470 --> 00:51:41.350
to the halfway house to check in.

00:51:41.350 --> 00:51:45.430
So I got a call one day saying get

00:51:45.430 --> 00:51:48.070
to the halfway house immediately.

00:51:48.070 --> 00:51:51.270
I said it's gonna take me two hours to get there

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:52.830
like it always does.

00:51:52.870 --> 00:51:54.670
So I started the trek.

00:51:54.670 --> 00:51:56.470
Two hours later I get to the halfway house

00:51:56.470 --> 00:51:58.550
and they took me into the conference room.

00:51:58.550 --> 00:52:01.270
It was the director of the halfway house,

00:52:01.270 --> 00:52:03.050
the bureau of prisons representative

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:05.070
had driven in from Baltimore.

00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:08.070
It was all this whole cast of characters.

00:52:08.070 --> 00:52:09.950
And they said that they had decided

00:52:09.950 --> 00:52:11.630
that they were gonna violate me

00:52:11.630 --> 00:52:13.910
and send me back to prison

00:52:13.910 --> 00:52:16.450
because I hadn't signed up for any

00:52:16.450 --> 00:52:18.430
of the mandatory classes

00:52:18.430 --> 00:52:21.050
and I had already been home for two weeks.

00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:24.010
And I said your mandatory classes

00:52:24.010 --> 00:52:26.410
are how to balance a checkbook,

00:52:26.410 --> 00:52:28.790
which I know how to balance a checkbook.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:31.290
How to write a resume, thank you very much,

00:52:31.290 --> 00:52:34.410
but I don't need your help to write my resume.

00:52:35.290 --> 00:52:40.290
And preventing suicide in prison.

00:52:40.330 --> 00:52:42.470
I said isn't that like closing the barn door

00:52:42.470 --> 00:52:45.170
after the animals already leave?

00:52:45.170 --> 00:52:48.050
You should give the anti-suicide class

00:52:48.050 --> 00:52:49.770
before people go to prison,

00:52:49.770 --> 00:52:52.530
not when they get home and they're happy that they're home.

00:52:53.690 --> 00:52:58.690
So I said, did you ever see that episode of The Simpsons?

00:53:01.090 --> 00:53:04.050
When Homer Simpson is sentenced

00:53:04.050 --> 00:53:07.090
to take a class on parenting

00:53:07.090 --> 00:53:11.890
and he walks into the class a little bit late

00:53:11.890 --> 00:53:14.090
and the teacher is saying,

00:53:14.090 --> 00:53:18.470
put a garbage can lid on the garbage can people.

00:53:18.470 --> 00:53:21.650
I can't stress that enough.

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:24.190
I said, is that what you're gonna teach me in your class?

00:53:24.190 --> 00:53:26.550
To put a garbage can lid on the garbage can?

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:29.150
I said, I'm not taking your class.

00:53:29.150 --> 00:53:32.030
And he says, step outside please.

00:53:32.030 --> 00:53:34.950
And I said, and I want permission to drive.

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:37.510
I'm not gonna spend two hours getting here

00:53:37.510 --> 00:53:40.510
and two hours getting back every single Tuesday

00:53:40.510 --> 00:53:42.270
to take a stupid drug test.

00:53:42.270 --> 00:53:43.750
You tell me I have to have a job

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:46.270
and then you take four hours out of my day.

00:53:46.270 --> 00:53:47.750
I'm not doing this anymore.

00:53:47.750 --> 00:53:49.710
I said, send me back to prison, I don't care.

00:53:49.710 --> 00:53:51.510
I only have two months to go anyway.

00:53:52.830 --> 00:53:56.330
So I went outside 45 minutes later, they came out

00:53:56.330 --> 00:53:59.590
and they said, all right, you don't have to take the classes

00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:01.430
and you can drive.

00:54:01.430 --> 00:54:03.630
And I said, that's more like it.

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:04.590
And I left.

00:54:05.790 --> 00:54:07.270
But I knew that they would back down.

00:54:07.270 --> 00:54:09.190
In your case, I'm gonna email you

00:54:09.190 --> 00:54:11.910
when I get back to the States on Sunday.

00:54:11.910 --> 00:54:13.590
I have some ideas.

00:54:13.710 --> 00:54:18.710
It sounds to me like they're picking on you

00:54:19.550 --> 00:54:22.070
because they think they can get away with it.

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:23.610
And that's just wrong.

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:25.310
It's wrong, it's unfair.

00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:27.470
And it's counterproductive, frankly.

00:54:27.470 --> 00:54:28.870
Espionage Act.

00:54:28.870 --> 00:54:29.710
Yep.

00:54:29.710 --> 00:54:31.050
Yeah.

00:54:31.050 --> 00:54:33.230
I have, as you might imagine,

00:54:33.230 --> 00:54:35.830
I have a thing about the Espionage Act.

00:54:35.830 --> 00:54:39.710
So the Espionage Act was written in 1917

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:42.930
to combat German saboteurs in World War I.

00:54:44.030 --> 00:54:48.390
It was updated once in 1950

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:50.970
after the Rosenbergs had been executed.

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:53.110
This is the gravest crime

00:54:53.110 --> 00:54:56.230
with which an American can be charged.

00:54:56.230 --> 00:55:02.690
And it sometimes carries with it the death penalty, right?

00:55:02.690 --> 00:55:04.210
Do you deserve the death penalty

00:55:04.210 --> 00:55:06.850
when you expose waste, fraud, abuse,

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:08.890
illegality or threats to the public health

00:55:08.890 --> 00:55:11.210
or public safety to the media?

00:55:11.210 --> 00:55:12.050
Come on.

00:55:13.050 --> 00:55:16.330
A precedent was set in the case of Tom Drake

00:55:16.330 --> 00:55:19.130
in the district of Maryland

00:55:19.130 --> 00:55:23.330
where the judge said that the government had to prove

00:55:23.330 --> 00:55:27.090
that Tom Drake had criminal intent

00:55:27.090 --> 00:55:31.170
that he intended to break the law

00:55:31.170 --> 00:55:33.530
when he made his revelations.

00:55:33.530 --> 00:55:35.370
He didn't have criminal intent

00:55:35.370 --> 00:55:37.290
and the case utterly fell apart.

00:55:38.930 --> 00:55:41.170
So when I went into court,

00:55:41.170 --> 00:55:43.330
my lawyer said to the judge,

00:55:43.330 --> 00:55:46.250
Your Honor, as per Judge Bennett

00:55:46.250 --> 00:55:48.650
in the Tom Drake case, criminal intent,

00:55:48.650 --> 00:55:50.530
Mr. Kiriakou had no criminal intent,

00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:52.650
and the judge interrupted and she says,

00:55:52.650 --> 00:55:55.570
I've chosen to not respect the precedent

00:55:55.570 --> 00:55:57.610
that was set in the Tom Drake case.

00:55:58.630 --> 00:55:59.990
And my lawyer said, Your Honor,

00:55:59.990 --> 00:56:01.650
are you saying that a person

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:04.350
can accidentally commit espionage?

00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:08.490
And she said, that's exactly what I'm saying.

00:56:08.490 --> 00:56:09.850
And then she looked at me and she said,

00:56:09.890 --> 00:56:14.410
Mr. Kiriakou, you either did it or you didn't do it,

00:56:14.410 --> 00:56:18.290
and you did it, I knew I was finished.

00:56:19.290 --> 00:56:21.050
And that was, I had only been arrested

00:56:21.050 --> 00:56:23.970
a day or two earlier, three days earlier.

00:56:23.970 --> 00:56:25.530
I was finished.

00:56:25.530 --> 00:56:27.570
But this is the problem with the Espionage Act

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:30.210
and Ed Snowden will tell you exactly the same thing.

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:32.930
Ed Snowden has repeatedly volunteered

00:56:32.930 --> 00:56:34.810
to return to the United States

00:56:34.810 --> 00:56:38.170
and has told the Justice Department repeatedly

00:56:38.170 --> 00:56:41.970
over the years that he is willing to go to prison

00:56:41.970 --> 00:56:44.010
if he can stand up in court

00:56:44.010 --> 00:56:47.050
and explain why he did what he did.

00:56:47.050 --> 00:56:49.410
And they said, forget it.

00:56:49.410 --> 00:56:52.650
You aren't gonna say anything in court.

00:56:52.650 --> 00:56:55.330
And that's why he stuck where he is,

00:56:55.330 --> 00:56:57.970
because they just will not let him

00:56:57.970 --> 00:56:59.750
provide an affirmative defense,

00:56:59.750 --> 00:57:01.810
or not even really an affirmative defense,

00:57:01.810 --> 00:57:05.730
but an explanation that he did what he did

00:57:05.730 --> 00:57:07.370
for the American people.

00:57:08.690 --> 00:57:10.130
Now one thing about the prosecution

00:57:10.130 --> 00:57:15.130
that's actually admirable if that's your bent,

00:57:15.570 --> 00:57:19.330
is that they learn from each of our cases,

00:57:19.330 --> 00:57:23.370
and they use that knowledge against the next whistleblower.

00:57:24.610 --> 00:57:29.210
In the case of Daniel Hale, the drone whistleblower,

00:57:29.210 --> 00:57:32.210
they raised reality's name in sentencing.

00:57:32.210 --> 00:57:33.150
They raised my name.

00:57:33.150 --> 00:57:35.610
I was there in the courtroom with Tom Drake,

00:57:35.610 --> 00:57:37.930
and they said that in my case,

00:57:37.930 --> 00:57:40.530
my sentence was far too short.

00:57:40.530 --> 00:57:42.250
They realized after the fact

00:57:42.250 --> 00:57:45.170
that they had agreed to a sentence that was far too short,

00:57:45.170 --> 00:57:50.170
I had 30 months and I should have had 120 months.

00:57:50.690 --> 00:57:53.450
And then because my sentence was so short,

00:57:53.450 --> 00:57:56.970
then Jeffrey Sterling's was accidentally too short,

00:57:56.970 --> 00:57:59.830
and then reality's was too short.

00:57:59.830 --> 00:58:03.050
And the judge said, no, no, no, no, you don't.

00:58:03.050 --> 00:58:05.610
That's not the way sentencing works.

00:58:05.610 --> 00:58:08.910
And besides, reality winner was in Georgia.

00:58:08.910 --> 00:58:11.570
We're talking about the Eastern District of Virginia,

00:58:11.570 --> 00:58:14.970
and Daniel Hale ended up getting 45 months,

00:58:14.970 --> 00:58:16.950
which is 45 months too long.

00:58:17.970 --> 00:58:21.010
So there's a real problem with this SB&Age Act,

00:58:21.010 --> 00:58:24.930
and the bigger problem is a political one, Anna.

00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:27.970
Can you imagine somebody running for Congress

00:58:27.970 --> 00:58:32.970
and saying, if elected, I'm going to make it harder

00:58:33.010 --> 00:58:35.450
to charge people with SB&Age.

00:58:35.450 --> 00:58:37.370
What district are you running in?

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:38.930
Nobody's gonna vote for that.

00:58:40.010 --> 00:58:43.370
What you need is somebody with the courage to say,

00:58:43.370 --> 00:58:45.130
look, this is the right thing to do.

00:58:45.130 --> 00:58:46.810
This law's being abused.

00:58:46.810 --> 00:58:49.210
It's being abused by the Justice Department.

00:58:49.210 --> 00:58:52.170
The Justice Department needs to be reined in,

00:58:52.170 --> 00:58:55.410
and we need to thank whistleblowers

00:58:55.410 --> 00:58:58.530
who are willing to risk everything they have

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:02.370
to report on fraud, abuse, illegality, et cetera, et cetera.

00:59:03.410 --> 00:59:05.170
The situation is flipped right now.

00:59:05.170 --> 00:59:07.170
Yeah, yeah.

00:59:07.170 --> 00:59:09.770
I know that we've covered a lot of ground.

00:59:09.770 --> 00:59:11.810
I do wanna ask Sonya again,

